POSTS_B = [
dict(slug='wood-vs-steel-pilings', cat='Docks, Pilings and Hoists', date='2026-09-20', hero='excavator-barge-timber-pilings',
 t='Wood vs. Steel Pilings: Choosing for Docks and Seawalls',
 h1='Wood vs. Steel Pilings: How to Choose for Docks, Seawalls and Piers',
 desc='Learn what pilings do, how wood and steel compare, why piling covers matter and what to expect when pilings are installed or removed.',
 takeaways=['Pilings carry loads down to firm ground and anchor docks, piers, seawalls and retaining walls.','Wood is a traditional, widely used choice for docks. Steel is stronger and used where loads and conditions are demanding.','Piling covers protect the top of a piling and extend its life.','Old or damaged pilings can be pulled and replaced, one at a time or as a whole structure.'],
 services=['pilings','docks','seawalls'], related=['floating-vs-fixed-docks','seawall-guide-metro-detroit','marine-steel-corrosion-welding-fabrication'],
 body='''
<p>Pilings are the hidden workhorses of waterfront construction. They hold up docks, anchor floating docks, support piers and boat hoists, and reinforce seawalls and retaining walls. When one fails, the structure above it does not stay put for long.</p>
<h2>What Pilings Do</h2>
<p>A piling is a long post driven into the bed or ground until it can carry load or resist movement. Depending on the project, pilings:</p>
<ul>
<li>Support a fixed dock or pier deck.</li>
<li>Hold floating docks in position while letting them rise and fall.</li>
<li>Carry boat hoists and boat house structures.</li>
<li>Help anchor and reinforce seawalls and retaining walls.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Wood Pilings</h2>
<p>Wood is a traditional choice for many docks, and installers know it well. Wood pilings suit many residential and lighter-duty jobs. The main issues are decay and wear, especially near the waterline and at the top, and damage from ice and boats.</p>
<h2>Steel Pilings</h2>
<p>Steel is stronger and is used where loads and conditions are demanding: seawall and retaining-wall pilings, heavier piers, marinas and commercial work. Steel needs corrosion protection over its life, which is why coatings and inspection matter. Our post on <a href="/blog/marine-steel-corrosion-welding-fabrication/">marine steel corrosion</a> explains the basics.</p>
<h2>Wood vs. Steel at a Glance</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Factor</th><th scope="col">Wood</th><th scope="col">Steel</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Typical use</th><td>Residential and lighter-duty docks</td><td>Seawalls, retaining walls, heavier piers, marinas</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Strength</th><td>Suits many dock loads</td><td>Stronger for demanding loads</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Main concern</th><td>Decay, wear, ice and boat damage</td><td>Corrosion protection</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Installation</th><td>Familiar and widely used</td><td>Typically needs heavier equipment</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Piling Covers</h2>
<p>A cover fits over the top of a piling to keep water and weather out of the most exposed part. Covers protect your investment and help extend its life, and they are a small line item compared with replacing a piling.</p>
<h2>Custom Pile Design</h2>
<p>Unusual loads, soft soils or demanding sites may call for custom pile design and engineering. Tell your contractor about the loads and conditions so the design fits.</p>
<h2>Removing Old or Damaged Pilings</h2>
<p>Pilings can be extracted, whether it is a single damaged piling or an entire structure. Careful removal limits disturbance to the surrounding area. Old pilings are sometimes removed as part of a dock replacement or <a href="/services/dredging/">dredging</a> project.</p>
<h2>Pilings and Permits</h2>
<p>Pilings are typically part of the permit for the dock or seawall they support. See <a href="/blog/michigan-permits-seawalls-docks-dredging/">Michigan permits for seawalls, docks and dredging</a>.</p>
<p>Stier’s Construction installs and removes steel and wood pilings and offers piling covers and custom pile design. See <a href="/services/pilings/">piling services</a> or <a href="/contact/">request a free quote</a>.</p>
''',
 faqs=[('How do I know if a piling is failing?','<p>Look for rot or heavy corrosion near the waterline, leaning, cracks, movement when the dock is loaded and visible wear at the top. A contractor can check for problems you cannot see.</p>'),
       ('Can I replace a single piling?','<p>Often yes. A single damaged piling can be removed and replaced without rebuilding the whole structure, depending on the condition of the rest.</p>')]),

dict(slug='how-to-choose-a-boat-hoist', cat='Docks, Pilings and Hoists', date='2026-09-20', hero='boat-house-hoist-tarped-boat',
 t='How to Choose a Boat Hoist: 4-Point, 8-Point, Elevator and More',
 h1='How to Choose a Boat Hoist: 4-Point, 8-Point, Elevator, PWC and Boat House Lifts',
 desc='Compare 4-point, 8-point, elevator, PWC and boat house hoists by boat length, hull support and dock layout to pick the right lift.',
 takeaways=['Boat length is the starting point: PWC lifts under 15 ft, 4-point up to about 25 ft, elevator up to about 30 ft, 8-point for 30 to 40 ft, and boat house hoists for boats up to 35 to 40 ft.','Weight, beam and hull shape matter as much as length.','Your dock layout and water depth affect which lift works.','Choose a lift with capacity to spare, and keep it maintained.'],
 services=['boat-hoists','docks','welding-fabrication'], related=['boat-hoist-maintenance-checklist','floating-vs-fixed-docks','seasonal-waterfront-maintenance-checklist'],
 body='''
<p>A boat hoist keeps your boat out of the water, which reduces wear, slows algae growth and makes access easy. But hoists are not one-size-fits-all. Here is how the main types compare, based on the size guidance we use.</p>
<h2>Size Guide by Lift Type</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Lift type</th><th scope="col">Typical boat</th><th scope="col">Good to know</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">PWC lift</th><td>Jet skis, WaveRunners, small watercraft under 15 ft</td><td>Lightweight and easy to operate; keeps the craft out of the water</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">4-point lift</th><td>Boats up to about 25 ft: runabouts, center consoles, fishing boats</td><td>Supports the hull at four contact points; compact, economical, simple to operate</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Elevator lift</th><td>Boats up to about 30 ft</td><td>Vertical lifting, often paired with a dock platform; suits tight waterfront areas and frequent launching</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">8-point lift</th><td>Boats 30 to 40 ft: larger cruisers and pontoon boats</td><td>Eight contact points; reduces hull stress, adds stability, suits wider beams</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Boat house hoist</th><td>Boats up to 35 to 40 ft</td><td>Built into a covered boat house; protects from weather and corrosion; good for seasonal or long-term storage</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Davits are another option for smaller craft. Ask us if they fit your setup.</p>
<h2>Beyond Length: What Else to Consider</h2>
<h3>Weight and Beam</h3>
<p>Length is only the starting point. A wide-beam pontoon boat or a heavy cruiser may need a different lift than a light runabout of the same length. Use your boat’s actual loaded weight, including fuel, gear and accessories, and pick a lift with capacity to spare.</p>
<h3>Hull Shape</h3>
<p>The lift has to support the hull where it is strong. Ask the contractor how the bunks or slings will contact your specific hull.</p>
<h3>Dock Layout and Water Depth</h3>
<p>The dock or structure carrying the lift has to handle its weight. Water depth and the bottom conditions affect installation. If your slip has silted in, see <a href="/blog/dredging-101-canals-marinas-boat-wells/">dredging 101</a>.</p>
<h3>Power and Controls</h3>
<p>Most lifts are motor-driven. Think about where power will come from and how the lift will be operated.</p>
<h3>Seasonal Use</h3>
<p>Some owners leave lifts in place year-round; others plan to raise or remove them for winter. Read <a href="/blog/michigan-winter-dock-seawall-damage/">how Michigan winters affect docks and seawalls</a>.</p>
<h2>Getting the Right Match</h2>
<p>Send us your boat’s make, length and weight, plus photos of your dock or slip, and we will help match the lift. Stier’s Construction installs boat house hoists, elevator, 4-point and 8-point lifts, PWC lifts and davits, and maintains them, including cable change-outs, motor service and welding repairs. See our <a href="/services/boat-hoists/">boat hoist page</a> or <a href="/contact/">request a free quote</a>.</p>
''',
 faqs=[('What is the difference between a 4-point and an 8-point lift?','<p>A 4-point lift supports the hull at four contact points and suits smaller boats. An 8-point lift spreads the load over eight points, which reduces hull stress and adds stability for larger boats and wider beams.</p>'),
       ('Can I install a hoist on my existing dock?','<p>Sometimes. The dock has to be designed for the lift’s weight and forces. Have a contractor review it first.</p>')]),

dict(slug='boat-hoist-maintenance-checklist', cat='Docks, Pilings and Hoists', date='2026-09-20', hero='welder-under-dock',
 t='Boat Hoist Maintenance Checklist: Cables, Motors and Welds',
 h1='Boat Hoist Maintenance Checklist: Cables, Motors, Frames and Welds',
 desc='A practical boat hoist maintenance checklist covering cables, motors, frames and welds, with tips for spring start-up and fall shutdown.',
 takeaways=['Cables, motors and welded frames are the three areas that most affect hoist safety and reliability.','Replace frayed, kinked or corroded cables and address uneven lifting promptly.','Check welds and supports for cracks and corrosion before the season.','Always follow your lift manufacturer’s instructions for service and winter care.'],
 services=['boat-hoists','welding-fabrication'], related=['how-to-choose-a-boat-hoist','seasonal-waterfront-maintenance-checklist','marine-steel-corrosion-welding-fabrication'],
 body='''
<p>A boat hoist carries thousands of pounds of boat over the water. A little maintenance goes a long way toward keeping it safe and preventing costly breakdowns. Use this checklist in spring before the first launch and again in fall. It is general guidance, so always follow your manufacturer’s instructions.</p>
<h2>Cables</h2>
<ul>
<li>Look for fraying, broken strands, kinks, flattening and corrosion along the full length.</li>
<li>Check where cables run over pulleys and terminate at fittings.</li>
<li>Watch for uneven lifting, which can mean one cable is stretched or a pulley is failing.</li>
<li>Replace worn or damaged cables promptly. Do not wait for the season to end.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Motor and Controls</h2>
<ul>
<li>Listen for grinding, slow response or new noises.</li>
<li>Inspect wiring, connections and switches for corrosion and wear.</li>
<li>Keep the motor clean, and have it inspected, cleaned, lubricated and serviced regularly to prevent breakdowns and extend its life.</li>
<li>Have a qualified person handle electrical repairs, especially around water.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Frame, Welds and Supports</h2>
<ul>
<li>Inspect welds for cracks, especially at joints and load points.</li>
<li>Look for rust, flaking coatings and pitting on frames and supports.</li>
<li>Check bolts and fasteners for looseness.</li>
<li>Check the pilings or dock structure supporting the lift. See <a href="/blog/wood-vs-steel-pilings/">wood vs. steel pilings</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Welding maintenance can repair or reinforce lift frames, supports and structural components, which helps keep the lift strong and reliable. Read our note on <a href="/blog/marine-steel-corrosion-welding-fabrication/">corrosion and marine steel</a>.</p>
<h2>Bunks, Slings and Contact Points</h2>
<ul>
<li>Check bunk carpet or padding, slings and straps for wear.</li>
<li>Make sure the boat sits evenly and the hull is supported where designed.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Spring Start-Up Routine</h2>
<ol>
<li>Inspect everything above, without a boat on the lift.</li>
<li>Cycle the lift up and down several times and watch for smooth, level travel.</li>
<li>Address problems before loading the boat.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Fall Shutdown Routine</h2>
<ol>
<li>Inspect and note any repairs for the off-season, when scheduling is easier.</li>
<li>Follow the manufacturer’s guidance on where to leave the lift for winter and how to protect it from ice. See <a href="/blog/michigan-winter-dock-seawall-damage/">Michigan winter damage</a>.</li>
<li>Store covers, canvas and removable parts as directed.</li>
</ol>
<h2>When to Call for Help</h2>
<p>Stier’s Construction handles boat hoist maintenance: cable change-outs, motor maintenance and welding repairs. See <a href="/services/boat-hoists/">boat hoist services</a> or <a href="/contact/">request a free quote</a>.</p>
''',
 faqs=[('How often should hoist cables be replaced?','<p>There is no single interval. Replace them when they show fraying, kinking, corrosion or uneven lifting, and follow your manufacturer’s guidance.</p>'),
       ('Can I do hoist maintenance myself?','<p>Visual inspection, cleaning and basic checks are reasonable for owners. Cable replacement, electrical work and welding are best left to qualified contractors.</p>')]),

dict(slug='dredging-101-canals-marinas-boat-wells', cat='Dredging', date='2026-09-20', hero='barge-excavator-canal',
 t='Dredging 101: Canals, Marinas and Boat Wells',
 h1='Dredging 101: When Canals, Marinas and Boat Wells Need It',
 desc='Learn the signs a canal, marina or boat well needs dredging, how dredging works, and what permits to expect on Lake St. Clair and the Detroit River.',
 takeaways=['Silt build-up reduces depth, makes boats touch bottom and limits navigation.','Dredging removes sediment and debris to restore proper depth.','Dredging on Great Lakes waters generally needs permits from EGLE, with federal review by the Army Corps.','Plan dredging together with seawalls, docks and hoists.'],
 services=['dredging','seawalls','docks'], related=['michigan-permits-seawalls-docks-dredging','great-lakes-water-levels-waterfront-property','how-to-choose-a-boat-hoist'],
 body='''
<p>Canals, marinas and boat wells collect sediment over time. Runoff, wave action and river currents all bring silt that settles where the water is calm. Eventually the water is too shallow for your boat. Dredging is how you get it back.</p>
<h2>What Is Dredging?</h2>
<p>Dredging removes sediment and debris from the bed of a waterway to restore depth and navigation. On residential canals and boat wells, that often means an excavator working from a barge or from shore. Larger marina jobs may use other equipment. Methods depend on the site, the amount of material and access.</p>
<h2>Signs You May Need Dredging</h2>
<ul>
<li>Your boat touches bottom at the dock or in the slip.</li>
<li>The propeller stirs up mud when you leave.</li>
<li>Boats that used to clear easily now wait for higher water.</li>
<li>Your canal or channel has visibly shoaled.</li>
<li>Your boat lift no longer lowers fully clear of the bottom.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Who Dredges?</h2>
<p>Homeowners with a boat well or slip, commercial waterfront properties and marinas all dredge. Canal, marina and boat well dredging are the main categories.</p>
<h2>Permits</h2>
<p>Dredging on Michigan’s Great Lakes bottomlands generally requires an EGLE permit, and federal review by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers may also apply. Sediment handling can be part of the review, and materials may need testing before they are moved or disposed of. Ask EGLE early and plan around the review time. Our <a href="/blog/michigan-permits-seawalls-docks-dredging/">permit guide</a> explains the process.</p>
<h2>Plan Dredging With Your Other Structures</h2>
<p>Dredging near a seawall or dock changes the depth at its base. The wall or pilings have to be designed for that depth. If you plan to dredge and rebuild a wall, coordinate them so the wall is designed for the new bottom. See our <a href="/blog/seawall-anatomy-tie-rods-caps-french-drains/">seawall anatomy</a> guide, and read how lake levels play into depth problems in <a href="/blog/great-lakes-water-levels-waterfront-property/">Great Lakes water levels</a>.</p>
<h2>What to Ask a Dredging Contractor</h2>
<ul>
<li>How will you access the site: from land, a barge or both?</li>
<li>How will you protect my seawall, pilings and dock?</li>
<li>What permits does this need, and who applies?</li>
<li>How will the removed material be handled?</li>
<li>How will you check final depth?</li>
</ul>
<p>Stier’s Construction provides canal, marina and boat well dredging, planned to minimize environmental impact. See <a href="/services/dredging/">dredging services</a> or <a href="/contact/">request a free quote</a>.</p>
''',
 faqs=[('How deep should my boat well be?','<p>Deep enough for your boat’s draft plus a margin, and for your lift to lower fully. Your boat’s specifications and your contractor can help set a target depth.</p>'),
       ('How often does a canal need dredging?','<p>It varies with sediment supply, currents and storms. Shallow water and grounding are the practical signals.</p>')]),

dict(slug='michigan-winter-dock-seawall-damage', cat='Michigan Waterfront Living', date='2026-09-20', hero='winter-dock-edge',
 t='How Michigan Winters Damage Docks and Seawalls',
 h1='How Michigan Winters Damage Docks and Seawalls (and How to Prepare)',
 desc='Ice, freeze-thaw and frozen ground stress docks, hoists and seawalls. Learn how winter damage happens and how to prepare your waterfront.',
 takeaways=['Ice pushes on walls, grips pilings and can lift or shift docks and hoists.','Freeze-thaw cycles widen cracks in caps, concrete and seams.','Drainage matters in winter too: trapped water freezes and expands.','Fall inspections and prep are cheaper than spring emergency repairs.'],
 services=['seawalls','docks','boat-hoists','concrete'], related=['seasonal-waterfront-maintenance-checklist','signs-your-seawall-is-failing','boat-hoist-maintenance-checklist'],
 body='''
<p>Michigan waterfronts spend part of the year frozen. Ice, shifting water levels and freeze-thaw cycles put stresses on docks, hoists and seawalls that summer never does. Here is how winter damage happens, and what you can do about it.</p>
<h2>How Winter Damages Waterfront Structures</h2>
<h3>Ice Pressure</h3>
<p>When ice forms and expands or moves with wind and current, it pushes on seawalls, pilings and docks. That can shift or bow walls and damage dock frames.</p>
<h3>Ice Grip on Pilings</h3>
<p>Ice can freeze to a piling. If the water level rises or falls, the ice can pull the piling up or twist it. Pilings that seem to have moved in spring may have been lifted by ice.</p>
<h3>Freeze-Thaw Cycles</h3>
<p>Water gets into small cracks in caps, concrete and seams, then freezes and expands. Repeated cycles widen the cracks. This is why small cracks are worth sealing or patching before winter. See <a href="/blog/waterfront-concrete-freeze-thaw/">waterfront concrete and freeze-thaw</a>.</p>
<h3>Trapped Water Behind Walls</h3>
<p>Water that cannot drain from behind a wall can freeze and add to the pressure on it. French drains help. See <a href="/blog/seawall-anatomy-tie-rods-caps-french-drains/">seawall anatomy</a>.</p>
<h3>Frozen Ground</h3>
<p>Frost can heave footings and posts. Municipal building departments in Michigan typically require footings to extend at least 42 inches below grade, though your local building official decides. This matters for decks and structures near the water.</p>
<h2>Fall Preparation Checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Walk the seawall.</strong> Note cracks, gaps, sinkholes and cap damage. Repair what you can before the freeze. See <a href="/blog/signs-your-seawall-is-failing/">seawall warning signs</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Clear drainage.</strong> Make sure French drains and surface drainage are working.</li>
<li><strong>Plan your dock.</strong> Decide whether sections come out for the winter, and follow the manufacturer’s guidance.</li>
<li><strong>Prepare your hoist.</strong> Follow your manufacturer’s winter instructions. See <a href="/blog/boat-hoist-maintenance-checklist/">hoist maintenance</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Protect pilings.</strong> Check piling covers and note anything that needs repair. See <a href="/blog/wood-vs-steel-pilings/">wood vs. steel pilings</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Take photos</strong> so you can compare in spring.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Spring Check-Up</h2>
<p>After the thaw, walk the shoreline again. Compare against your fall photos. Look for new leaning, gaps, heaved pilings and cracked caps. Schedule repairs early, since spring is a busy season for waterfront contractors.</p>
<h2>Winter Work Is Possible</h2>
<p>Marine work does not stop entirely in winter. Our <a href="/projects/">project photos</a> include jobs done in snow and on ice. Ask about scheduling for your project.</p>
<p>Stier’s Construction handles seawall repair, dock work, hoist maintenance and concrete. <a href="/contact/">Request a free quote</a> or call <a href="tel:+15867037214">586-703-7214</a>.</p>
''',
 faqs=[('Should I remove my dock for winter?','<p>It depends on the dock type and exposure. Follow the manufacturer’s guidance and ask your contractor. Some docks are designed to stay; others are removed or partly removed.</p>'),
       ('Can winter damage show up months later?','<p>Yes. Small cracks and shifts often show up in spring as the ground thaws. Compare against fall photos.</p>')]),

dict(slug='great-lakes-water-levels-waterfront-property', cat='Michigan Waterfront Living', date='2026-09-20', hero='barge-open-water',
 t='Great Lakes Water Levels and Your Waterfront Property',
 h1='Great Lakes Water Levels and What They Mean for Your Waterfront Property',
 desc='Record highs and later drops on Lake St. Clair show how water levels affect seawalls, docks and hoists. Learn how to plan for both extremes.',
 takeaways=['Lake St. Clair set record high levels in 2019, and again in 2020, causing erosion and flooding.','By April 2022 the lake had dropped about 20 inches below the April 2020 record, showing how fast conditions can change.','High water stresses seawalls and floods low structures; low water exposes docks and silts up slips.','The Army Corps’ Detroit District publishes monitoring and forecasts.'],
 services=['seawalls','docks','dredging','boat-hoists'], related=['floating-vs-fixed-docks','dredging-101-canals-marinas-boat-wells','michigan-permits-seawalls-docks-dredging'],
 body='''
<p>Great Lakes water levels are not steady. They rise and fall with rain, snowmelt, evaporation and long-term weather patterns. If you own property on Lake St. Clair or the Detroit River, those swings show up on your seawall, your dock and your boat lift.</p>
<h2>Recent History on Lake St. Clair</h2>
<p>The U.S. Army Corps of Engineers, Detroit District, reported that Lake St. Clair set record monthly high levels in 2019, tied its January record in 2020 and set new monthly records through 2020. In August 2020, the lake surpassed the previous year’s record. The Corps warned that erosion and flooding were significant in many locations.</p>
<p>Then conditions changed. According to the Corps’ April 2022 summary, reported by local media, Lake St. Clair was about 20 inches below the April 2020 record. In roughly two years, the lake went from record highs to well below them, though still above its long-term average.</p>
<h2>What High Water Does</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Raises wave energy against seawalls</strong> and can overtop low walls, washing out soil behind them.</li>
<li><strong>Floods low areas</strong> such as patios, docks and ground-level structures.</li>
<li><strong>Shrinks freeboard:</strong> the distance between the water and the top of the dock or wall.</li>
<li><strong>Increases erosion,</strong> especially at wall ends and unprotected areas.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What Low Water Does</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Exposes bottom conditions</strong> and makes shallow slips and boat wells obvious. See <a href="/blog/dredging-101-canals-marinas-boat-wells/">dredging 101</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Raises dock height above the water,</strong> especially for fixed docks. See <a href="/blog/floating-vs-fixed-docks/">floating vs. fixed docks</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Changes how hoists and boats sit,</strong> and can make launching harder.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Designing for Both Extremes</h2>
<p>Because levels swing both ways, good waterfront design allows for a range:</p>
<ul>
<li>Set wall and dock elevations with high water in mind.</li>
<li>Choose dock types that suit variable levels.</li>
<li>Keep drainage working so water behind a wall does not build up when levels fall quickly.</li>
<li>Plan for dredging if silt is limiting depth.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Permits After High Water</h2>
<p>EGLE reported that in 2020, shore protection permit requests were up nearly three times from five years earlier, and it worked to expedite reviews prioritized by risk. Application steps still apply. See <a href="/blog/michigan-permits-seawalls-docks-dredging/">our permit guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Where to Watch Water Levels</h2>
<p>The Army Corps’ Detroit District publishes monitoring, data and forecasts for the Great Lakes on its <a href="USACE_LEVELS" target="_blank" rel="noopener">water levels page</a>.</p>
<p>Stier’s Construction builds and repairs seawalls, docks and hoists and dredges for Lake St. Clair and Detroit River properties. <a href="/contact/">Request a free quote</a>.</p>
''',
 faqs=[('What are Lake St. Clair’s current water levels?','<p>Levels change monthly. Check the Army Corps’ Detroit District water levels page for current data and forecasts.</p>'),
       ('Should I build my seawall higher because of record high water?','<p>Height is a design decision based on your site, exposure and permits. Ask your contractor and EGLE about your project.</p>')]),
]
